# Placeholders for deferred features are inert and say so

Sometimes a plan keeps a product affordance **visible** while its implementation is deferred, so the
idea is not silently lost. That placeholder has one job: show that the feature is coming without ever
appearing to work.

## Required

- **Non-interactive.** Disable the control so a tap or click changes nothing. There must be no state
  to flip, because a control that flips and forgets reads as a bug or, worse, as a saved preference.
- **Fixed at the inactive value.** A placeholder switch is hard-coded off. Do not read or write a
  preference, and do not add storage "ready for later" — the next agent will find the wiring and
  assume the feature half-exists.
- **Says it in words.** Put a short line next to the control stating the feature is not available
  yet, through i18n like any other user-facing copy. Disabled styling alone asks the user to discover
  the limitation by tapping.

## Don't

- Don't ship an enabled control whose handler is a no-op, a `TODO`, or a toast.
- Don't persist a value the product cannot honor. A remembered setting that has no effect is a
  promise the app breaks on every launch.
- Don't hide the real state behind optimistic UI — nothing is pending, so nothing should spin.
- Don't cite the deferral's plan, detail, or step number in a code comment
  ([`comments-future-forward`](/.cursor/rules/comments-future-forward.mdc)). The durable home for the
  decision is the detail doc under `docs/proposals/`; the file only needs to be honest about today.

## Where the reasoning lives

The placeholder answers "what does the user see". The **open questions** — tier, defaults, storage,
which surfaces get it — belong in the deferral's detail doc, which is what a later agent picks up.
If the deferral also constrains future work, give it a rule so the constraint is enforced rather than
remembered.

